### Experiment 13 – HTTP Web Server and Client Communication

Devices required

* 1 Cisco 2911 Router

* 1 Cisco 2960 Switch

* 1 Server

* 1 PC (PC1 optional)

* 4 Copper Straight-Through cables

Step 1: Create topology

Drag the devices into the workspace.

Connect:

* Router G0/0 → Switch

* Server FastEthernet0 → Switch

* PC0 FastEthernet0 → Switch

* Optional PC1 → Switch

Step 2: Configure router

Click Router → CLI → enter:

```text
enable
configure terminal
interface g0/0
ip address 192.168.1.1 255.255.255.0
no shutdown
exit
copy running-config startup-config
```

If asked for the destination filename, press Enter.

Step 3: Configure server IP

Server → Desktop → IP Configuration.

| Parameter       | Value         |
| --------------- | ------------- |
| IP Address      | 192.168.1.100 |
| Subnet Mask     | 255.255.255.0 |
| Default Gateway | 192.168.1.1   |

Step 4: Enable HTTP

* Click Server.

* Select Services.

* Select HTTP.

* Set HTTP Service to ON.

Step 5: Create a webpage

In the HTTP window, edit `index.html` and replace its contents with:

```html
<html>
<head>
<title>Computer Networks Lab</title>
</head>
<body bgcolor="lightblue">
<h1 align="center">
WELCOME TO COMPUTER NETWORKS LAB
</h1>
<hr>
<h2 align="center">
HTTP Web Server Successfully Configured
</h2>
<p align="center">
Department of Computer Science and Engineering
</p>
<p align="center">
Cisco Packet Tracer Laboratory Experiment
</p>
</body>
</html>
```

Click Save.

Step 6: Configure PC0

PC0 → Desktop → IP Configuration.

| Parameter       | Value         |
| --------------- | ------------- |
| IP Address      | 192.168.1.10  |
| Subnet Mask     | 255.255.255.0 |
| Default Gateway | 192.168.1.1   |

Step 7: Test connectivity

PC0 → Desktop → Command Prompt:

```cmd
ping 192.168.1.100
```

Step 8: Open website

* PC0 → Desktop → Web Browser.

* Enter:

```text
http://192.168.1.100
```

3\. Click Go.

You should see:

* WELCOME TO COMPUTER NETWORKS LAB

* HTTP Web Server Successfully Configured

The webpage hosted on the server should open successfully on PC0.

### Experiment 14 – FTP File Transfer Between Client and Server

Devices required

* 1 PC

* 1 Server

* 1 Cisco 2960 Switch

* 2 Copper Straight-Through cables

Step 1: Create topology

Connect:

* PC0 → Switch

* Server0 → Switch

Step 2: Configure PC0

PC0 → Desktop → IP Configuration.

| Parameter   | Value         |
| ----------- | ------------- |
| IP Address  | 192.168.1.2   |
| Subnet Mask | 255.255.255.0 |
| Gateway     | 192.168.1.1   |

Step 3: Configure Server0

Server0 → Desktop → IP Configuration.

| Parameter   | Value         |
| ----------- | ------------- |
| IP Address  | 192.168.1.10  |
| Subnet Mask | 255.255.255.0 |
| Gateway     | 192.168.1.1   |

Step 4: Test connection

PC0 → Desktop → Command Prompt:

```cmd
ping 192.168.1.10
```

Step 5: Enable FTP

* Click Server0.

* Select Services.

* Select FTP.

* Turn FTP Service ON.

Step 6: Create FTP user

Enter:

| Parameter | Value    |
| --------- | -------- |
| Username  | student  |
| Password  | cisco123 |

Click Add.

Step 7: Create a server file

In the FTP service window, create:

* Filename: `network.txt`

* Contents:

```text
Welcome to FTP Server
Cisco Packet Tracer Lab
Networking Practical
```

Save the file.

Step 8: Connect from PC0

PC0 → Desktop → Command Prompt:

```cmd
ftp 192.168.1.10
```

Enter the login details:

```text
Username: student
Password: cisco123
```

Step 9: Display available files

```cmd
dir
```

You should see:

```text
network.txt
```

Step 10: Download the file

```cmd
get network.txt
```

Expected output:

```text
Receiving network.txt...
Transfer Complete
```

Step 11: Upload a file (optional)

Create `student.txt` on the client if the Packet Tracer client supports local file creation, then run:

```cmd
put student.txt
```

Expected output:

```text
Transfer Complete
```

Step 12: Exit FTP

```cmd
bye
```

Step 13: Observe FTP packets

* Switch to Simulation Mode.

* Click Edit Filters.

* Enable FTP and TCP.

* Click Capture/Forward repeatedly.

Observe:

```text
TCP SYN
   ↓
TCP SYN-ACK
   ↓
TCP ACK
   ↓
FTP Login
   ↓
Username and Password
   ↓
File Request
   ↓
File Transfer
   ↓
TCP ACK
   ↓
FIN / ACK
```

FTP login succeeds and the client downloads `network.txt` from the server. FTP uses TCP, with control communication on port 21.

### Experiment 15 – TCP vs UDP Basic Communication Simulation

Devices required

* 1 Cisco 2960 Switch

* 1 PC

* 1 Server

* 2 Copper Straight-Through cables

Step 1: Create topology

Connect:

* PC0 → Switch

* Server0 → Switch

Step 2: Configure PC0

PC0 → Desktop → IP Configuration.

| Parameter   | Value         |
| ----------- | ------------- |
| IP Address  | 192.168.1.2   |
| Subnet Mask | 255.255.255.0 |
| Gateway     | 192.168.1.1   |

Step 3: Configure Server0

Server0 → Desktop → IP Configuration.

| Parameter   | Value         |
| ----------- | ------------- |
| IP Address  | 192.168.1.10  |
| Subnet Mask | 255.255.255.0 |
| Gateway     | 192.168.1.1   |

Step 4: Test connectivity

On PC0 → Command Prompt:

```cmd
ping 192.168.1.10
```

### Part A – TCP communication

Step 5: Enable HTTP

* Click Server0.

* Open Services → HTTP.

* Turn HTTP ON.

Step 6: Access HTTP page

PC0 → Desktop → Web Browser.

Enter:

```text
http://192.168.1.10
```

The default webpage should appear.

Step 7: Observe TCP packets

* Switch to Simulation Mode.

* Open Edit Filters.

* Enable TCP and HTTP.

* Click Capture/Forward repeatedly.

Observe:

```text
TCP SYN
   ↓
TCP SYN-ACK
   ↓
TCP ACK
   ↓
HTTP GET
   ↓
HTTP Response
   ↓
TCP FIN
   ↓
TCP ACK
```

This demonstrates TCP's three-way handshake before data communication.

### Part B – UDP communication using DNS

Step 8: Enable DNS service

* Click Server0.

* Select Services → DNS.

* Turn DNS ON.

Create a DNS record:

| Parameter | Value        |
| --------- | ------------ |
| Name      | server.com   |
| Address   | 192.168.1.10 |

Click Add.

Step 9: Configure DNS on PC0

PC0 → Desktop → IP Configuration.

Set DNS Server:

```text
192.168.1.10
```

Keep the PC's IP address and subnet mask unchanged.

Step 10: Test DNS

PC0 → Desktop → Web Browser.

Enter:

```text
http://server.com
```

The browser should resolve the domain and display the server webpage.

Step 11: Observe UDP packets

* Switch to Simulation Mode.

* Open Edit Filters.

* Enable UDP and DNS.

* Click Capture/Forward.

Observe:

```text
DNS Query
    ↓
DNS Response
```

DNS uses UDP for this query. After the name is resolved, the HTTP webpage communication uses TCP.

TCP communication shows a three-way handshake, while DNS communication using UDP shows a query and response without a TCP handshake.

